  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "Human {speech} contains more than 2,000 different sounds, from the common “m” and “a” to the rare {clicks} of some {southern African} languages.But why are certain sounds more common than others? A {ground-breaking} , five-year study shows that {diet} -related changes in human {bite} led to new speech sounds that are now found in half the world's languages.",
      "cn": "人类 语言 包含2000多种不同的声音，从常见的“m”和“a”到 非洲南部 某些语言中罕见的 吸气音 。但为什么某些声音比其他声音更常见呢？一项历时五年的 开拓性 研究表明，人类因 饮食 导致的 咬合 变化催生了新的发音，而这些发音如今已存在于世界上一半的语言之中。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "Cultural factors have received {priority} in language studies as their influences might seem {straightforward} .However, speech sounds can change in a rather {passive} way.More than 30 years ago, the scholar Charles Hockett noted that speech sounds called labiodentals ( (唇齿音) ), such as “f” and “v”，were more {typical} in the languages of societies that ate softer foods.Now a team of researchers led by Damián Blasi at the University of Zurich, Switzerland, has found how and why this trend {arose} .",
      "cn": "语言研究向来 优先 考虑文化因素，因为它们的影响看似 直观明了 。然而，语音可以以一种相当 被动的 方式发生变化。30多年前，学者查尔斯·霍克特就注意到，在食用较软食物的社会，诸如“f”和“v”的唇齿音在其语言中更为 常见 。现在，瑞士苏黎世大学的达米安·布拉西带领的研究团队发现了这一趋势的 产生 过程和原因。"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "They discovered that the {arches} of upper and lower front teeth of ancient human adults were aligned ( (对齐) ), making it hard for the {speakers} to produce labiodentals, which are formed by touching the lower lip to the upper teeth.Later, our {jaws} changed to an overbite ( (上包齿) ) {structure} , making it easier to produce such sounds, which is also why {abnormal} bite patterns like underbite ( (反颌) ) or crowded teeth would lead to unclear pronunciation of certain sounds.",
      "cn": "他们发现，古代成年人的上下门牙 牙弓 是相互对齐的，这使得 说话者 很难发出下唇碰触上齿形成的唇齿音。后来，我们的 颌骨 演变成了上包齿 结构 ，从而更容易发出唇齿音，这也解释了为何反颌或牙齿拥挤等 异常 咬合模式会导致某些发音不清晰。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "The team showed that this change in bite was connected with the {development} of agriculture in the Neolithic period.Food became easier to {chew} at this point.We didn't have to eat {rough} food like {bamboo} and {raw meat} anymore.The {jawbone} didn't have to do as much work and so didn't grow to be so large.The advent ( (出现) ) of food processing brought about {finer} food like {bread} and {rice} , {prompting} the {accommodation} of our {organ} structures even further.",
      "cn": "该团队发现，这种咬合方式的变化与新石器时代的农业 发展 有关。在这一时期，食物变得更容易 咀嚼 了。我们不必再吃 竹子 、 生肉 等 粗硬的 食物。 下颚骨 无需再承担那么多咀嚼工作，因此也就不会长得那么大了。食品加工业的出现带来了 面包 、 米饭 等更 精细的 食物，进一步 促使 我们的 器官 结构做出 适应 性变化。"
    },
    {
      "num": "05",
      "en": "{Subsequent analysis} of a language {database} also confirmed that there was a global change in the sound of languages after the Neolithic age, with the use of “f” and “v” increasing {remarkably} during the last few thousand years.These sounds are still not found in the languages of many hunter-gatherer ( (狩猎采集者) ) peoples today.",
      "cn": "对语言 数据库 的 后续分析 也证实，新石器时代之后，各种语言的发音发生了全球性的变化。在过去几千年里，“f”和“v”的使用 显著 增加。如今，在许多狩猎采集者群体所使用的语言中，仍然找不到这些音。"
    },
    {
      "num": "06",
      "en": "This research {overturns} the popular view that all human speech sounds were present when human beings evolved around 300,000 years ago.“The set of speech sounds we use has not necessarily remained {stable} since the appearance of human beings, but rather the {huge} variety of speech sounds that we find today is the product of a complex {interplay} of things like {biological} change and cultural evolution,”said Steven Moran, a member of the research team.",
      "cn": "这项研究 推翻 了广为流行的观点，即大约30万年前，人类经由演化诞生之时，所有人类语音就已经存在。研究团队的成员史蒂文·莫兰说：“自人类出现以来，我们使用的语音体系不一定总是保持 稳定 ，相反，我们如今所发现的种类 繁 多的语音是 生物 变化和文化演进等诸多复杂因素 相互作用 的产物。” 5NoOncib/NjiX02BpMIzP4m+378Hw1xTRxUdPaP/PFjKUFq4K2G7tp6ZLYoCE601yW1W39VMdLmovFdSHs4nVQ=="
    }
  ],
